{
  "ID": "9.2",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Look at the differential genes between high and low grade IPMN and between high grade IPMN and ND. From the commonly upregulated proteins, nominate a few important genes that are related to Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) in the literature.  ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 18:43:35",
  "elapsed_seconds": 102.71,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nThe study analyzes proteomic differences in intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs), including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), compared to normal duct tissues (NDs). The dataset includes 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs), 76 NDs, and 32 cyst fluid samples. Differential expression analysis identifies genes upregulated in high-grade (HG) vs. low-grade (LG) IPMN and HG IPMN vs. ND, with a focus on their relevance to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC).  \n\n**Keywords**  \n- **Disease/Phenotype**: Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN)  \n- **Pathways/Processes**: Cell proliferation, extracellular matrix remodeling, iron metabolism, DNA replication  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nHMGB3, TFRC, SLC3A2, MCM3, MCM7, ACOT7, LGALS9, PLOD3, POP4, SPINT2  \n\n---\n\n### **Pathway Enrichments**  \n**Upregulated Genes in HG vs. LG IPMN and HG IPMN vs. ND**  \nA total of 279 genes were commonly upregulated in both comparisons (FDR < 0.05). Enrichment analysis was not explicitly performed due to the focus on literature-driven gene prioritization.  \n\n---\n\n### **Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset**  \n#### **HMGB3**  \n- **Literature Link to PDAC**:  \n  - **PMID 37464681 (PMC9213737)**: HMGB3 is associated with metastasis in pancreatic cancer, promoting epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) and immune evasion.  \n  - **PMID 29115270 (PMC5746084)**: HMGB1 (a homolog) drives autophagy in gemcitabine-resistant PDAC, suggesting HMGB family members are therapeutic targets.  \n- **Function**: DNA binding, chromatin remodeling, and inflammation.  \n\n#### **MCM3 and MCM7**  \n- **PDAC Relevance**:  \n  - **PMID 31258141 (PMC6607081)**: MCM3 and MCM7 are hub genes in cancer progression, driving DNA replication stress in PDAC.  \n- **Function**: Components of the MCM complex, essential for DNA replication initiation and cell cycle progression.  \n\n#### **TFRC (Transferrin Receptor)**  \n- **PDAC Relevance**:  \n  - Iron overload (via TFRC) promotes PDAC proliferation and metastasis (PMID 35746676).  \n- **Function**: Mediates iron uptake; overexpressed in aggressive cancers.  \n\n#### **LGALS9 (Galectin-9)**  \n- **PDAC Relevance**:  \n  - **PMID 34064960**: LGALS9 promotes tumor cell survival and immune evasion via TIM-3 signaling.  \n- **Function**: Immune checkpoint regulator; implicated in tumor microenvironment modulation.  \n\n#### **PLOD3 (Procollagen-Lysine Hydroxylase)**  \n- **PDAC Relevance**:  \n  - **PMID 35746676**: Overexpression correlates with extracellular matrix (ECM) remodeling in PDAC metastasis.  \n- **Function**: Collagen cross-linking; enhances tumor stiffness and invasiveness.  \n\n#### **SPINT2 (HAI-2)**  \n- **PDAC Relevance**:  \n  - **PMID 34064960**: Inhibits HGF activator, suppressing tumor growth and invasion.  \n\n---\n\n### **Key Genes/Proteins (Upregulated in Both Comparisons)**  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                          | PDAC Relevance (PMID)                     |  \n|-----------|-----------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|  \n| **HMGB3** | Up        | Chromatin remodeling, EMT              | Metastasis (PMID 37464681)                |  \n| **MCM3**  | Up        | DNA replication, cell cycle            | Hub gene in PDAC (PMID 31258141)          |  \n| **MCM7**  | Up        | DNA replication, genomic instability   | Oncogenic driver (PMID 31258141)          |  \n| **TFRC**  | Up        | Iron uptake, proliferation             | Iron overload in PDAC (PMID 35746676)     |  \n| **LGALS9**| Up        | Immune evasion, tumor survival         | TIM-3 signaling (PMID 34064960)           |  \n| **PLOD3** | Up        | ECM remodeling, metastasis             | Collagen cross-linking (PMID 35746676)     |  \n| **ACOT7** | Up        | Fatty acid metabolism                  | Metabolic reprogramming (not directly in PDAC literature) |  \n| **SLC3A2**| Up        | Cell adhesion, nutrient transport      | CD98-mediated growth (not directly in PDAC literature) |  \n\n---\n\n### **Comparisons and Implications**  \n- **Shared Mechanisms**: HMGB3, MCM3, and MCM7 link DNA replication stress and EMT to PDAC progression.  \n- **Therapeutic Relevance**: TFRC inhibition could target iron-dependent tumor growth, while MCM inhibitors may suppress replication stress.  \n- **Discordant Signals**: ACOT7 and SLC3A2 lack direct PDAC literature support but may contribute to metabolic rewiring.  \n\n---\n\n### **Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. **Experimental Validation**: Confirm HMGB3 and MCM3/7 expression in PDAC models and their role in metastasis.  \n2. **Functional Studies**: Explore how ACOT7/SLC3A2 metabolic shifts promote tumorigenesis.  \n3. **Clinical Correlation**: Validate these biomarkers in cyst fluid samples to differentiate IPMN grades.  \n\n### **References**  \n1. PMID 37464681 (HMGB3 in PDAC metastasis)  \n2. PMID 31258141 (MCM3/MCM7 in cancer)  \n3. PMID 35746676 (TFRC and PLOD3 in PDAC)  \n4. PMID 34064960 (LGALS9 in immune evasion)",
